<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.12.1: Using the Slope Formula</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00261</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.12.1: Using the Slope Formula</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a0f24faf-4dfb-4e50-89b8-2ffaf5739389</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<para>Use the slope formula, \( m =\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}\), to find the slope of the line through the given points.</para>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\( (1, -2) \) and \( (3, 4) \)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\( m= 3 \)</para>
    <para>First, name \( (1, −2) \) as point 1 and \( (3, 4) \) as point 2.</para>
    <para>\( (x_1, y_1) \) and \( (x_2, y_2) \)</para>
    <para>\( (1,−2) \) and \( (3, 4) \)</para>
    <para>Use the slope formula.</para>
    <para><span>\( m=\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1} \) 
      </span></para>
    <para>Substitute the values: \( y_2 = 4, y_1 = -2, x_2 = 3, x_1 = 1 \).</para>
    <para>\( m=\frac{4-(-2)}{3-(1)} \)</para>
    <para>Simplify.</para>
    <para>\( m=\frac{6}{2} \)</para>
    <para>\( m=3 \)</para>
  </solution></exercise>

<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\( (-2, -3) \) and \( (-7, 4) \)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\( m=-\frac{7}{5} \)</para>
    <para>First, name \( (−2, −3) \) as point 1 and \( (−7, 4) \) as point 2.</para>
    <para>\( (x_1, y_1) \) and \( (x_2, y_2) \)</para>
    <para>\( (−2,−3)\) and \( (−7, 4) \)</para>
    <para>Use the slope formula.</para>
    <para><span>\( m=\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1} \)</span></para>
    <para>Substitute the values: \( y_2 = 4, y_1 = −3, x_2 = −7, x_1 = −2 \).</para>
    <para>\( m= \frac{4-(-3)}{-7-(-2)} \)</para>
    <para>Simplify.</para>
    <para>\( m= \frac{7}{-5} \)</para>
    <para>\( m= -\frac{7}{5} \)</para>
  </solution></exercise>

</section>
